          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Socrates</strong><br>\nSocrates was born around 470 BC in Athens, Greece. His father was a sculptor, and his mother was a midwife. Unlike many other Greek philosophers, Socrates did not come from a noble or wealthy family, which likely influenced his unique perspective on Athenian society. As a young man, he received the typical education of the time, learning to read, write, and study poetry and music.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Socratic Method</strong><br>\nThe Socratic Method, named after Socrates, is a form of cooperative argumentative dialogue between individuals. This method involves asking and answering questions to stimulate critical thinking and to illuminate ideas. Socrates used this technique to dissect the meanings and assumptions behind common beliefs, often leading to a better understanding of the subject.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Philosophy and Beliefs</strong><br>\nSocrates believed that the way to gain knowledge was through persistent questioning. He asserted that through examining life, one could discover the truth about moral issues. Unlike the Sophists, who claimed to know about the divine, Socrates humbly acknowledged his own ignorance. He considered this self-awareness of ignorance a crucial step in acquiring true wisdom.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
