            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Impact on Athens</strong><br>\nSocrates spent his life in Athens during its golden age and interacted with many influential Athenians. His style of questioning not only won him admirers but also created many enemies who felt threatened by his challenges to traditional beliefs and the status quo. His method and ideas helped lay the foundational work for Western philosophy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Accusations</strong><br>\nIn 399 BC, Socrates was brought to trial on charges of impiety and corrupting the youth of Athens. His accusers argued that his teachings undermined the traditional gods of Athens and the moral fiber of its young citizens. Socrates defended his philosophy and method but did little to appease the jury.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Trial of Socrates</strong><br>\nDuring his trial, Socrates utilized his method of questioning to demonstrate the lack of true knowledge amongst his accusers. He stood firm on his principles, arguing for the pursuit of virtue and the good life above all else. His defense is captured in Plato's \"Apology,\" which outlines his arguments and philosophy.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
