          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Death Sentence and Hemlock</strong><br>\nDespite his defense, Socrates was found guilty and sentenced to death by drinking a poisonous brew of hemlock. He accepted his fate with composure, believing that a true philosopher should not fear death. His death became one of the most famous moments in philosophical history, symbolizing the quest for truth regardless of personal cost.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Socrates' Legacy</strong><br>\nSocrates left no written records, but his ideas survived through the works of his students, like Plato and Xenophon. His teachings and method became a cornerstone in the development of Western philosophy, influencing countless philosophers from his day to the present.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Socrates in Literature</strong><br>\nPlato's dialogues are the primary source of what we know about Socrates. In these works, Socrates' character often serves as a vehicle for Plato's own philosophical ideas. Other contemporary writers also mentioned Socrates, sometimes with admiration and other times with less favorable impressions, reflecting the controversy he inspired during his life.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
